/**

@page benchmarks_exahype2_euler_smooth_fd4 Convergence Tests with Fourth-Order Finite Differences (Euler Equations)

\tableofcontents

This benchmark checks that the fourth-order Finite Differences with
Runge-Kutta time stepping (@ref exahype2.api.rkfd4) converge with fourth
order. It uses the setup of the Finite Volume convergence tests
(@ref benchmarks_exahype2_euler_smooth_setup): the Euler equations over
@f$ [-0.1,0.1]^d @f$, where the density follows one period of a sine wave
along x, while velocity @f$ u=1 @f$ (along x) and pressure @f$ p=1 @f$ are
constant. The exact solution is the initial density profile advected with
the velocity, i.e.

@f$ \rho(x,t) = 1 + 0.2 \sin \left( 2 \pi \frac{x - t + 0.1}{0.2} \right). @f$

By default, the boundary conditions prescribe this exact solution outside of
the domain. Pass --periodic for periodic boundary conditions instead.


## Prepare benchmark

Configure and build Peano with

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
--enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

and ensure that the Python environment variables are set:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=<path-to-peano4-repository>/src
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


## Run

The script smooth-convergence-test-fd4.py generates and builds the code for
one mesh width. All PDE terms are passed to the solver as C++ snippets, so
there is no user code to write. Build and run one executable per mesh width,
each in a directory of its own:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
for volume_size in 0.0075 0.0025 0.00083; do
  python3 smooth-convergence-test-fd4.py --output-directory run-$volume_size --volume-size $volume_size --verbosity text
  (cd run-$volume_size && ./smooth-convergence-test-fd4)
done
python3 compute-errors.py run-0.0075 run-0.0025 run-0.00083
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

convergence-study.py does the same in one go and checks the order (it is part of the
integration test, skill exahype2:integration-test):

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 convergence-study.py --work-directory <directory-outside-of-the-repository>
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The three volume sizes yield 27, 81 and 243 grid cells per axis, as each mesh
cell hosts a patch of 9x9 grid cells. python3 smooth-convergence-test-fd4.py
--help lists further options, such as the Runge-Kutta order, fixed time step
sizes or three dimensions.

compute-errors.py compares the density of the last snapshot of each run with
the exact solution, and prints the maximum and the root mean square error
plus the convergence orders between subsequent runs. The plot files hold
the patches' offsets with seven significant digits only. As this would
dominate the errors on the finer meshes, the script snaps the offsets onto
the regular mesh.


## Results

With the default settings, i.e. Runge-Kutta of order four, adaptive time
step sizes with a CFL constant of 0.5, no dissipation, Dirichlet boundary
conditions and the end time 0.05, we obtain in 2d:

| Grid cells per axis | Time steps | Max error of density | RMS error of density | Order (max) | Order (RMS) |
|--------------------:|-----------:|---------------------:|---------------------:|------------:|------------:|
| 27                  |         64 | 3.82e-05             | 2.10e-05             |             |             |
| 81                  |        190 | 4.77e-07             | 2.59e-07             | 3.99        | 4.00        |
| 243                 |        566 | 5.89e-09             | 3.18e-09             | 4.00        | 4.01        |

The errors decrease with the fourth power of the mesh width. As the time
step size is proportional to the mesh width, this confirms the order of the
Runge-Kutta scheme, too.

*/
